      },
      "worked": "The original equations require $t\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=t+\\frac1t\\\\y&=t-\\frac1t\\\\x^2-y^2&=\\left(t+\\frac1t\\right)^2-\\left(t-\\frac1t\\right)^2\\\\x^2-y^2&=4\\end{align*}$$\nEvery point on this hyperbola has $(x+y)(x-y)=4$, so $x+y\\ne0$. Set $t=\\dfrac{x+y}{2}$; then $\\dfrac1t=\\dfrac{x-y}{2}$, recovering both original coordinates.\nHence $x^2-y^2=4$ describes the whole hyperbola, with no additional curve restrictions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
